1
00:00:00,001 --> 00:00:08,600
This state-of-the-art M109A6 Paladin is a 32-ton self-propelled cannon system.

2
00:00:10,220 --> 00:00:15,000
Operation Iraqi Freedom marked the first time the $2.7 million Paladin,

3
00:00:15,580 --> 00:00:19,260
with its 155-millimeter cannon, had been fired in battle.

4
00:00:19,261 --> 00:00:33,261
Developed by United Defense, the M109A6 proved itself to be a powerful combination of sophisticated digital technology and devastating firepower.

5
00:00:36,120 --> 00:00:40,760
The soldiers were exceptionally pleased with the performance of the paladin.

6
00:00:41,480 --> 00:00:45,460
And there were questions as to could the paladin keep up with the maneuver force.

7
00:00:45,960 --> 00:00:48,000
The answer was absolutely.

8
00:00:49,260 --> 00:00:54,460
The M109A6 Paladin may look like its predecessor, the M109,

9
00:00:54,461 --> 00:00:59,420
but with cutting-edge technology, the U.S. Army considers today's

10
00:00:59,421 --> 00:01:03,900
Paladin a new weapon system, a cannon with a brain.

11
00:01:04,340 --> 00:01:07,500
The heart of the Paladin system is its integrated onboard fire

12
00:01:07,501 --> 00:01:10,820
control system, comprised of the inertial navigation system,

13
00:01:11,140 --> 00:01:14,620
the GPS, the onboard ballistic computer and weapons controller,

14
00:01:14,900 --> 00:01:17,360
and its ability to process digital communications.

15
00:01:17,361 --> 00:01:22,080
With a touch of a button and without soldiers ever leaving the vehicle,

16
00:01:22,860 --> 00:01:25,920
this howitzer will automatically take aim and fire.

17
00:01:27,480 --> 00:01:31,280
Because of the built-in navigation and automatic fire control systems,

18
00:01:31,840 --> 00:01:34,400
the crew doesn't have to see the target to hit it.

19
00:01:35,640 --> 00:01:39,280
When the paladin receives a fire mission with designated coordinates,

20
00:01:39,780 --> 00:01:42,620
the onboard computer calculates a firing solution

21
00:01:42,621 --> 00:01:45,940
and automatically unlocks and points the cannon

22
00:01:45,941 --> 00:01:50,300
so the crew can fire the first projectile within 60 seconds.

23
00:01:51,260 --> 00:01:53,320
Because of the responsiveness of the fires

24
00:01:53,321 --> 00:01:56,280
and the accuracy of the Paladin made it a very lethal weapon.

25
00:01:56,740 --> 00:02:00,980
I can shoot a target as long as it's at least 500 meters away from me.

26
00:02:01,720 --> 00:02:04,780
So from 500 meters to 30 Ks, we can shoot it.

27
00:02:06,140 --> 00:02:08,780
Today's Paladin brings two strategic capabilities

28
00:02:08,781 --> 00:02:10,260
to the modern field artillery.

29
00:02:12,720 --> 00:02:14,720
First, responsiveness.

30
00:02:15,940 --> 00:02:21,480
Responsiveness is incredibly important for the soldiers that are supported by cannon fires.

31
00:02:22,140 --> 00:02:25,320
When the maneuver elements or the infantrymen call for fire support,

32
00:02:25,760 --> 00:02:29,300
the Paladin system can provide that in a near real-time environment.

33
00:02:29,720 --> 00:02:33,940
Earlier versions of M109s could not provide that timely, responsive fire.

34
00:02:34,940 --> 00:02:37,480
And the second, survivability.

35
00:02:37,920 --> 00:02:42,700
On the earlier M109s, it took up to 15 minutes to respond to fire orders

36
00:02:42,701 --> 00:02:45,140
because soldiers had to exit the vehicles

37
00:02:45,141 --> 00:02:47,300
to manually find their position on the battlefield,

38
00:02:48,300 --> 00:02:49,660
putting their lives at risk.

39
00:02:50,880 --> 00:02:53,320
The soldiers never have to leave the new Paladin.

40
00:02:53,860 --> 00:02:55,240
It shoots and scoots.

41
00:02:55,900 --> 00:02:58,920
It fires quickly and moves at 40 miles an hour,

42
00:02:59,700 --> 00:03:01,840
greatly reducing exposure to enemy fire.

43
00:03:02,900 --> 00:03:05,400
With the Paladin, if I've got to reload a howitzer

44
00:03:05,401 --> 00:03:06,580
to keep its combat load,

45
00:03:06,840 --> 00:03:08,560
the only people who really are on the ground

46
00:03:08,561 --> 00:03:09,660
is the ammo guys.

47
00:03:10,080 --> 00:03:11,400
It's alleviated a lot of checks.

48
00:03:12,700 --> 00:03:16,140
The Paladin can shoot four 100-pound rounds a minute,

49
00:03:16,720 --> 00:03:18,840
accommodating a wide variety of munitions,

50
00:03:19,620 --> 00:03:22,220
including cluster bombs and high-explosive rounds.

51
00:03:22,780 --> 00:03:26,780
But this smart cannon will soon be firing a smart bomb.

52
00:03:27,640 --> 00:03:30,000
Currently being developed by Raytheon Missile Systems

53
00:03:30,001 --> 00:03:32,980
is the precision-guided weapon Excalibur.

54
00:03:34,280 --> 00:03:37,360
Excalibur will be the Army's first artillery projectile

55
00:03:37,361 --> 00:03:38,500
guided by GPS.

56
00:03:38,501 --> 00:03:48,400
As the round exits the cannon itself, it begins to receive and track satellite emissions from the GPS satellites.

57
00:03:48,720 --> 00:03:53,140
Prior to launch, it's been provided with the target information for the target of interest for that mission.

58
00:03:54,120 --> 00:03:58,120
It can be detonated above the ground for more area-type targets,

59
00:03:58,260 --> 00:04:01,400
or it can actually be detonated as it comes in contact with a target.

60
00:04:02,020 --> 00:04:06,760
The 40-pound Excalibur will be able to steer around objects to locate its target

61
00:04:06,761 --> 00:04:09,260
at distances within 50 kilometers

62
00:04:09,261 --> 00:04:13,600
and can be programmed to change its trajectory in mid-flight.

63
00:04:15,620 --> 00:04:18,560
The Excalibur is intended for urban environments

64
00:04:18,561 --> 00:04:20,720
where there's risk of collateral damage.

65
00:04:22,660 --> 00:04:24,260
So you can take out the target

66
00:04:24,261 --> 00:04:26,540
without taking out the building, the school bus,

67
00:04:26,920 --> 00:04:30,380
the civilians that are in the same block that the target set is in.

68
00:04:30,680 --> 00:04:33,720
The deployment of the Excalibur will mark the first time

69
00:04:33,721 --> 00:04:36,060
smart weapons will be used in cannon warfare.

70
00:04:36,760 --> 00:04:40,060
It will revolutionize cannon artillery.

71
00:04:40,061 --> 00:04:43,700
It just came a little slower than bombs because of the environment.

72
00:04:44,440 --> 00:04:47,340
It needs to survive in coming out of the cannon tube.

73
00:04:48,480 --> 00:04:52,000
The Excalibur will be distributed to the Army in 2006.

74
00:04:53,160 --> 00:04:55,220
It will make the Paladin a superweapon,

75
00:04:55,740 --> 00:04:59,420
one of the most lethal incarnations of a battlefield technology

76
00:04:59,421 --> 00:05:02,520
that has its origins in the 13th century.

77
00:05:02,521 --> 00:05:11,700
The Chinese were the first to cram gunpowder, which was invented in the 9th century, into

78
00:05:11,701 --> 00:05:16,500
a long tube, load it with an iron projectile, and thus create the first cannon.

79
00:05:17,420 --> 00:05:21,060
There wasn't a grand revolution once gunpowder was invented.

80
00:05:22,100 --> 00:05:29,180
They were used as fireworks, they were used as loud, scary kind of noisemakers, but not

81
00:05:29,181 --> 00:05:30,880
really effective siege weapons.

82
00:05:30,881 --> 00:05:43,160
But by about the mid-13th century, first quarter of the 14th century, gunpowder and cannon make its way to Europe.

83
00:05:43,880 --> 00:05:48,020
The purpose of early cannons was principally as a siege weapon.

84
00:05:48,720 --> 00:05:56,800
In an effort for some princes to consolidate power, they had to have a way to reduce those tall, standing, high-walled castles.

85
00:05:56,801 --> 00:06:07,440
And the way to do that is to drag out these siege cannons, put them in place, and very methodically pelt the base of these ancient castles and cause them to collapse.

86
00:06:09,100 --> 00:06:16,880
These siege battles tended to last for months, if not years, allowing gunners time to literally build cannons on the battlefield.

87
00:06:18,100 --> 00:06:25,840
Even with all this practice, producing cannons in the 13th and 14th centuries was a mysterious mix of science and art.

88
00:06:26,800 --> 00:06:31,200
The folks that ran those things were considered a little different.

89
00:06:32,160 --> 00:06:36,140
And they were very clannish. They stuck together.

90
00:06:37,060 --> 00:06:41,300
The guns are extremely expensive, so they are very personalized.

91
00:06:41,580 --> 00:06:42,720
They took great pride in them.

92
00:06:43,620 --> 00:06:49,100
As you look at the guns, you can see that they carry the emblems of either the nobleman or the king.

93
00:06:49,101 --> 00:07:00,080
Some of the guns that you'll see have dolphins, sea dragons, all those things that are, at the time, considered good omens, good luck charms.

94
00:07:01,840 --> 00:07:08,120
Interestingly, the first cannon makers were bellmakers, because they understood the technology of working with metal.

95
00:07:08,121 --> 00:07:17,720
They would have a sand mold made and then pour the liquid metal in and cast the cannon that whole way

96
00:07:17,721 --> 00:07:20,940
so that it would cool from the outside in.

97
00:07:21,560 --> 00:07:26,380
And then when it was finished, you broke the mold apart and you had just a solid tube.

98
00:07:27,240 --> 00:07:32,540
What you did then was they took a metal bore and bored out the center of the tube

99
00:07:32,541 --> 00:07:36,240
to make your actual gun tube so that you would have that firing channel.

100
00:07:38,120 --> 00:07:43,340
Over the next two centuries, bronze, which was lighter and easier to move around the battlefield than iron,

101
00:07:43,860 --> 00:07:45,640
came to dominate the cannon industry.

102
00:07:46,300 --> 00:07:50,280
This despite the fact that iron cannons were ten times cheaper to produce than bronze.

103
00:07:51,100 --> 00:07:54,820
The first person to fully utilize lightweight cannons on the battlefield

104
00:07:54,821 --> 00:07:59,260
was the 16th century Swedish monarch Gustavus Adolphus.

105
00:08:00,100 --> 00:08:05,040
He lightened up the guns so that the guns could move along with the infantry

106
00:08:05,041 --> 00:08:09,140
and provide fire support to the infantry on the battlefield,

107
00:08:09,740 --> 00:08:11,740
which was a big advance also.

108
00:08:13,080 --> 00:08:15,980
Cannoneers would fire a cannon by placing the cannonball

109
00:08:15,981 --> 00:08:18,400
and a powder charge inside a flannel casing.

110
00:08:19,200 --> 00:08:21,760
The cannoneer would then muzzleload the explosive.

111
00:08:22,720 --> 00:08:26,640
Once that happened, the cannoneer would take a goose quill full of powder

112
00:08:26,641 --> 00:08:30,800
and stick it through an opening at the base of the cannon, igniting the charge.

113
00:08:31,620 --> 00:08:36,560
By the late 14th century, cannons started firing exploding shells.

114
00:08:37,300 --> 00:08:41,180
Made of stone or bronze, the shells were joined together by hoops

115
00:08:41,181 --> 00:08:43,620
and exploded by use of a primitive fuse.

116
00:08:44,620 --> 00:08:49,080
They were usually filled with an incendiary mixture and mostly used against forts.

117
00:08:50,480 --> 00:08:54,860
Most cannons could fire a stone or iron ball roughly 1,000 yards.

118
00:08:55,860 --> 00:08:59,760
But since the cannon bores were smooth, the aim wasn't always true.

119
00:09:00,800 --> 00:09:05,920
A smoothbore can't control the projectile because the shell is just pushed through the barrel.

120
00:09:07,400 --> 00:09:10,740
Rifleboard cannons produced a stabilizing spin on the shell.

121
00:09:11,940 --> 00:09:16,320
Once the fuse was ignited, the gas pressure inside the cannon barrel expanded

122
00:09:16,321 --> 00:09:22,260
and secured the shell inside the rifling grooves, improving its accuracy and range.

123
00:09:24,360 --> 00:09:27,320
Rifling originally had little to do with accuracy.

124
00:09:27,880 --> 00:09:33,260
The rifling was simply a groove cut through the cannon that allowed the powder to collect.

125
00:09:33,340 --> 00:09:36,540
It was a place to collect the spent powder to get it out of the way of the bore,

126
00:09:36,920 --> 00:09:38,560
and then they would clean it out and ream it out.

127
00:09:39,080 --> 00:09:42,980
Discovered eventually that with the right twist, it made it more accurate.

128
00:09:44,680 --> 00:09:49,440
Large cannons saw their first dominant role surface in the 1588 Spanish Armada.

129
00:09:50,120 --> 00:09:54,840
The British and Spanish fleets battled for almost two weeks in the English Channel.

130
00:09:55,380 --> 00:09:57,520
Each side had roughly 40 warships,

131
00:09:58,180 --> 00:10:00,020
but it was the size of the British cannons

132
00:10:00,021 --> 00:10:02,700
that eventually overwhelmed the Spanish fleet.

133
00:10:04,060 --> 00:10:05,920
Naval battles would never be the same,

134
00:10:06,520 --> 00:10:10,060
as large cannons became the weapon of choice on the high seas.

135
00:10:10,940 --> 00:10:14,520
Because of their mobility, smaller cannons prevailed on the battlefield.

136
00:10:15,700 --> 00:10:17,540
And in the winter of 1775,

137
00:10:17,541 --> 00:10:21,660
a Massachusetts bookstore owner altered the course of the Revolutionary War.

138
00:10:22,660 --> 00:10:24,860
Henry Knox recovered 50 British cannons

139
00:10:24,861 --> 00:10:28,240
and brought them to Boston from the captured Fort Ticonderoga,

140
00:10:29,100 --> 00:10:30,940
forcing the British to retreat from Boston.

141
00:10:32,560 --> 00:10:33,700
Several months later, of course,

142
00:10:33,980 --> 00:10:36,780
the colonists declared their independence from Great Britain.

143
00:10:38,580 --> 00:10:40,640
Following their success in the Revolutionary War,

144
00:10:41,160 --> 00:10:43,680
cannons played a larger role in the Mexican-American War.

145
00:10:43,860 --> 00:10:46,380
But it wasn't until 1854

146
00:10:46,381 --> 00:10:49,560
that a British hydraulic engineer named William Armstrong

147
00:10:49,561 --> 00:10:51,580
solved the problem of gas leaks.

148
00:10:51,660 --> 00:10:54,660
and made a major advancement in improving accuracy.

149
00:10:55,860 --> 00:10:59,900
He developed a process that would heat and shrink the barrels over the cannons

150
00:10:59,901 --> 00:11:04,140
to improve the strength in the area where the greatest internal pressure occurred.

151
00:11:06,560 --> 00:11:10,040
Armstrong and other cannon makers coupled this advance with breech loading,

152
00:11:10,480 --> 00:11:12,820
and finally, they rifle-boarded their cannons.

153
00:11:13,180 --> 00:11:19,200
The problem up until the 19th century was that cannon were made out of bronze,

154
00:11:19,201 --> 00:11:23,400
and if you make something out of bronze, you can't rifle it.

155
00:11:23,840 --> 00:11:25,660
So you needed something hard.

156
00:11:28,460 --> 00:11:31,280
Iron cannons had always been cheaper to produce than bronze,

157
00:11:31,480 --> 00:11:35,100
and now with the problem of leaks solved, they regained favor.

158
00:11:37,200 --> 00:11:41,280
By the 19th century, the Industrial Revolution allowed foundry workers

159
00:11:41,281 --> 00:11:44,360
and metallurgists to jumpstart a cannon production boom.

160
00:11:44,361 --> 00:11:52,060
Probably the most significant foundry was the West Point Foundry in Cold Springs, New York.

161
00:11:53,400 --> 00:12:01,160
It was one of four sites selected by President James Madison following the War of 1812 to produce cannons for the U.S.

162
00:12:04,340 --> 00:12:08,620
This foundry produced the majority of cannons used by the Union Army in the Civil War.

163
00:12:09,500 --> 00:12:13,380
As is often the case, war pushed the technological envelope.

164
00:12:14,360 --> 00:12:17,960
and the civil war era propelled cannon technology on several fronts

165
00:12:19,160 --> 00:12:25,320
first of these obviously was smokeless powder you need to have a propellant that that when it sends

166
00:12:25,321 --> 00:12:30,840
that projectile out of the tube it doesn't give away the position of the tube the second invention

167
00:12:30,841 --> 00:12:35,640
of course was was the recoil cylinder remember before the invention of the recoil cylinder when

168
00:12:35,641 --> 00:12:41,080
a gun fired the whole gun moved back and you had to roll the gun back in battery and put it back

169
00:12:41,081 --> 00:12:43,320
into position before you could shoot it again.

170
00:12:43,321 --> 00:12:47,920
Well, when that happened, you were never assured that the tube was back in the same spot each

171
00:12:47,921 --> 00:12:48,920
time.

172
00:12:48,921 --> 00:12:53,080
And it wasn't until you had the ability to have the gun recall down a slide and move

173
00:12:53,081 --> 00:12:56,260
back into position after each round that you were able to do that.

174
00:12:56,261 --> 00:13:04,040
The third invention was the invention of really effective and reliable fuses.

175
00:13:04,041 --> 00:13:08,760
Fuses had been around for almost 150 years by the time the Civil War started.

176
00:13:08,761 --> 00:13:15,300
The fuses were wooden tubes, several inches long, that were rammed into the projectile and lit right before firing.

177
00:13:16,220 --> 00:13:20,360
The fuse would launch the projectile as well as detonate the shell over the target area.

178
00:13:22,000 --> 00:13:25,240
Having the fuse detonate at the desired time was always troublesome.

179
00:13:25,760 --> 00:13:31,560
This was solved with the invention of the percussion fuse, which detonated upon impact.

180
00:13:31,561 --> 00:13:35,520
By the end of the 19th century,

181
00:13:36,260 --> 00:13:39,120
cannons would become the centerpiece of battlefield artillery.

182
00:13:39,960 --> 00:13:43,180
Out of this elevated role would come larger weapons

183
00:13:43,181 --> 00:13:45,680
to wreak havoc on unsuspecting civilians,

184
00:13:46,240 --> 00:13:49,160
as well as terrified soldiers.

185
00:13:53,780 --> 00:13:55,920
At the beginning of World War I,

186
00:13:56,600 --> 00:13:58,780
cannons had a commanding presence on the battlefield.

187
00:13:58,781 --> 00:14:05,960
They were now being mass produced, but while their numbers increased, their accuracy did

188
00:14:05,961 --> 00:14:06,960
not.

189
00:14:06,961 --> 00:14:11,540
Countries from all over the world wanted quicker firing, more accurate cannons to gain the

190
00:14:11,541 --> 00:14:13,180
upper hand.

191
00:14:13,181 --> 00:14:19,020
But without effective recoil systems, accuracy wouldn't improve.

192
00:14:19,021 --> 00:14:24,780
Unfortunately, established recoil systems didn't allow cannons to keep their position

193
00:14:24,781 --> 00:14:27,360
once fired.

194
00:14:27,361 --> 00:14:30,741
springs were the best method used in cannon recoil systems.

195
00:14:30,742 --> 00:14:32,460
But even they wore out quickly

196
00:14:32,461 --> 00:14:36,647
due to the excessive force generated by these cannons.

197
00:14:36,648 --> 00:14:39,360
With the world watching, the French

198
00:14:39,361 --> 00:14:43,320
unveiled a historic recoil system that changed the way cannons were built.

199
00:14:44,420 --> 00:14:48,332
They came up with what they call the hydropneumatic system.

200
00:14:48,333 --> 00:14:50,520
In fact, this is what they put on

201
00:14:50,521 --> 00:14:53,780
their famous French 75, and it was their secret weapon.

202
00:14:53,781 --> 00:14:57,480
where other cannons used springs.

203
00:14:58,140 --> 00:15:00,060
The French 75 used cylinders,

204
00:15:00,640 --> 00:15:03,240
one filled with oil, the other filled with nitrogen.

205
00:15:04,300 --> 00:15:08,200
When fired, the cylinder filled with nitrogen would compress.

206
00:15:09,100 --> 00:15:11,460
This actually pushed the cannon back into battery,

207
00:15:11,800 --> 00:15:13,240
making it ready to fire again.

208
00:15:14,380 --> 00:15:18,000
The French 75 was the forerunner of modern field artillery.

209
00:15:19,320 --> 00:15:21,640
Its hydropneumatic system also allowed the cannon

210
00:15:21,641 --> 00:15:24,000
to return to its original position gently,

211
00:15:24,680 --> 00:15:26,380
decreasing the wear and tear on the cannon.

212
00:15:29,020 --> 00:15:32,900
And they could do up to 12 rounds a minute

213
00:15:32,901 --> 00:15:34,320
out of the French 75.

214
00:15:35,760 --> 00:15:38,160
They used to say they would have one in the gun,

215
00:15:38,300 --> 00:15:40,840
six in the air, when they were really firing fast.

216
00:15:44,920 --> 00:15:47,680
The cannon weighed 2,700 pounds,

217
00:15:47,800 --> 00:15:51,280
was about 18 feet long, almost 10 feet wide.

218
00:15:51,640 --> 00:15:54,900
stood six feet high and was protected by a gun shield.

219
00:15:55,940 --> 00:15:58,520
The 16-pound projectiles were breech-loaded

220
00:15:58,521 --> 00:16:01,520
and had a range of roughly 7,500 yards.

221
00:16:02,260 --> 00:16:04,120
You got a real killer now on the battlefield.

222
00:16:04,780 --> 00:16:06,060
And in the First World War,

223
00:16:06,360 --> 00:16:08,520
that's going to become very, very, very important.

224
00:16:10,060 --> 00:16:12,360
The French 75's recoil system

225
00:16:12,361 --> 00:16:15,600
set off an international game of how'd they do that?

226
00:16:16,340 --> 00:16:17,540
In fact, in World War I,

227
00:16:17,940 --> 00:16:20,100
when they brought it over to America,

228
00:16:20,101 --> 00:16:23,260
we couldn't take the gun apart to find out how it worked

229
00:16:23,261 --> 00:16:25,140
because it was a French national secret.

230
00:16:25,440 --> 00:16:26,300
It leaked out.

231
00:16:26,740 --> 00:16:29,180
There's no way you can keep something like that under wraps.

232
00:16:30,340 --> 00:16:33,180
And pretty soon all Europe's armies

233
00:16:33,181 --> 00:16:35,880
are going to have something like the French 75.

234
00:16:37,360 --> 00:16:39,600
This new cannon was the standard bearer

235
00:16:39,601 --> 00:16:41,340
in the early days of World War I.

236
00:16:42,300 --> 00:16:44,320
But as the war moved into the trenches,

237
00:16:44,920 --> 00:16:47,320
the French 75 became less effective.

238
00:16:47,321 --> 00:16:51,820
In fact, the French 75, like all cannons that launched projectiles

239
00:16:51,821 --> 00:16:55,820
with high trajectories, fired over the heads of soldiers who

240
00:16:55,821 --> 00:16:56,820
had burrowed into the ground.

241
00:16:59,820 --> 00:17:03,820
The Germans were the first to counteract this problem.

242
00:17:03,821 --> 00:17:07,820
They built the biggest cannons to date, two 42 centimeter

243
00:17:07,821 --> 00:17:12,320
howitzers called Big Bertha that smashed the fort at Liège,

244
00:17:12,321 --> 00:17:14,820
Belgium in August 1914.

245
00:17:14,821 --> 00:17:21,580
No one had ever seen that before, that you could take a siege gun, which was in effect

246
00:17:21,581 --> 00:17:26,880
a big siege mortar, and smash these very modern forts.

247
00:17:27,080 --> 00:17:32,280
The whole function was to have it go up to about 20,000 feet, turn over and come down

248
00:17:32,281 --> 00:17:35,440
and penetrate deeply into the earth.

249
00:17:37,040 --> 00:17:42,640
Named for the daughter of the Krupp Gun Foundry founder, the Big Bertha was able to launch

250
00:17:42,641 --> 00:17:44,832
1,800-pound shells at its enemy.

251
00:17:44,833 --> 00:17:48,600
Because of its weight, Big Bertha had to be transported

252
00:17:48,601 --> 00:17:53,400
piecemeal behind five motor tractors and assembled on site by a crane.

253
00:17:53,600 --> 00:17:58,100
The Germans brought her forward, and she was one of the first motorized artillery units.

254
00:17:58,620 --> 00:18:06,280
It took them about a day to emplace the battery and then a day to get the range settled in.

255
00:18:06,281 --> 00:18:12,780
But once they did, they smashed each fort in turn and destroyed them all.

256
00:18:14,060 --> 00:18:15,480
But there was a problem.

257
00:18:16,740 --> 00:18:22,500
Firing Big Bertha damaged its recoil system to such a degree that she could only fire a few times a day.

258
00:18:23,980 --> 00:18:30,200
Nevertheless, Big Bertha was a precursor to weapons that would become a staple on the front lines of World War I.

259
00:18:30,680 --> 00:18:34,100
Redwood guns are pretty effective in World War I in that they are big.

260
00:18:34,101 --> 00:18:41,760
And remember, the front in the First World War is fixed, so it doesn't move very far.

261
00:18:42,460 --> 00:18:49,380
And when you bring up a 14-inch gun that fires a projectile that weighs 1,800 pounds, that's pretty effective.

262
00:18:49,580 --> 00:18:52,840
When an 1,800-pound round lands on top of your head, you know it.

263
00:18:54,360 --> 00:18:57,260
Several armies developed similar weapons to Big Bertha,

264
00:18:57,920 --> 00:19:02,180
causing the Germans to up the ante by producing an even bigger cannon.

265
00:19:02,181 --> 00:19:08,060
From the Big Bertha, Krupp then developed what they called the Paris Gun.

266
00:19:08,620 --> 00:19:14,980
And this was an attempt by the Germans to sow confusion and panic in the French capital in 1917.

267
00:19:17,540 --> 00:19:23,820
At 142 tons, the Paris Gun more than tripled the weight of Big Bertha.

268
00:19:24,500 --> 00:19:28,640
She fired a 150-pound shell more than 75 miles.

269
00:19:29,620 --> 00:19:33,900
The Paris gun had an unprecedented 110-foot-long barrel.

270
00:19:36,020 --> 00:19:41,600
From March to July 1918, the Paris gun was hidden in the forest of Crepe,

271
00:19:42,080 --> 00:19:45,640
on the outskirts of Paris, and aimed at the city.

272
00:19:46,480 --> 00:19:49,080
Once fired, the French had no idea what hit them.

273
00:19:49,720 --> 00:19:54,580
In fact, when it was first fired into Paris, the French thought a gas works had blown up.

274
00:19:55,080 --> 00:19:57,160
They didn't realize that they were being shelled.

275
00:19:57,161 --> 00:20:03,020
The secondary thing was, well, maybe they were being bombed from the air.

276
00:20:04,020 --> 00:20:07,020
Such a high-flying Zeppelin, perhaps, that they couldn't see it.

277
00:20:07,960 --> 00:20:10,360
After a while, though, it became apparent they were being shelled.

278
00:20:10,580 --> 00:20:13,980
This was one of the first guns that actually got something up into the upper atmosphere.

279
00:20:15,120 --> 00:20:18,460
And so it would get up there and then just kind of skip on the way down.

280
00:20:19,220 --> 00:20:24,200
The interesting thing about the Paris gun was that it was so big

281
00:20:24,201 --> 00:20:28,120
that it had to have a superstructure above the gun

282
00:20:28,121 --> 00:20:30,080
because when the gun fired, it whipped.

283
00:20:30,900 --> 00:20:33,540
As the Allies pushed Germany on other fronts, however,

284
00:20:34,020 --> 00:20:36,180
the Germans had to rethink the usefulness

285
00:20:36,181 --> 00:20:38,660
of the cumbersome and costly Paris gun.

286
00:20:39,240 --> 00:20:41,060
They ceased firing the Paris gun,

287
00:20:41,280 --> 00:20:43,720
took it back to Krupp, and dismantled it.

288
00:20:46,920 --> 00:20:49,020
While the Paris gun met its demise,

289
00:20:49,840 --> 00:20:52,300
large cannons on ships and in harbor defenses

290
00:20:52,301 --> 00:20:54,120
were duking it out throughout the war.

291
00:20:54,200 --> 00:20:58,000
Naval and coastal cannons fought on equal terms

292
00:20:58,001 --> 00:21:01,240
until coastal artillery seized the upper hand

293
00:21:01,241 --> 00:21:02,880
with the development of a weapon

294
00:21:02,881 --> 00:21:05,520
that seemingly vanished as quickly as it appeared.

295
00:21:06,640 --> 00:21:09,820
The advantage of a disappearing gun

296
00:21:09,821 --> 00:21:12,960
is that if your enemy is restricted

297
00:21:12,961 --> 00:21:15,060
to flat trajectory fire,

298
00:21:15,660 --> 00:21:17,500
as most naval weapons were

299
00:21:17,501 --> 00:21:18,860
at the time of their introduction,

300
00:21:19,440 --> 00:21:21,000
then the disappearing gun

301
00:21:21,001 --> 00:21:23,860
is raised up over an earth and concrete

302
00:21:23,861 --> 00:21:28,940
parapet fired, then the force of its recoil carries it back

303
00:21:28,941 --> 00:21:30,940
under or behind that parapet.

304
00:21:32,340 --> 00:21:35,840
This worked because the enemy only saw the cannon for the few

305
00:21:35,841 --> 00:21:38,840
seconds it was firing, so he couldn't get a fix on it.

306
00:21:39,800 --> 00:21:41,900
Also, the parapet protected the cannon.

307
00:21:42,760 --> 00:21:45,740
But naval cannons eventually answered this problem.

308
00:21:46,780 --> 00:21:51,700
The demise of disappearing carriages was really brought

309
00:21:51,701 --> 00:21:56,540
about by the improvement in technology to naval artillery

310
00:21:56,541 --> 00:22:01,760
that allowed them to indulge in high-trajectory fire.

311
00:22:03,620 --> 00:22:06,120
The Germans would benefit from that technology.

312
00:22:07,060 --> 00:22:09,740
Their love of large cannons would show itself again

313
00:22:09,741 --> 00:22:11,360
following World War I.

314
00:22:12,700 --> 00:22:15,480
They would begin plans for a killing machine

315
00:22:15,481 --> 00:22:18,880
that would make the Paris gun look like a pea shooter.

316
00:22:21,700 --> 00:22:28,620
As World War II began, Germany continued to push the envelope in its development of large cannons.

317
00:22:29,620 --> 00:22:33,380
While Big Bertha and the Paris gun were only destructive in limited instances,

318
00:22:34,140 --> 00:22:38,000
the Germans still felt large railway cannons were a key part of their arsenal.

319
00:22:39,560 --> 00:22:42,780
During World War II, the Germans developed a gun called Dora.

320
00:22:43,220 --> 00:22:46,000
It was an 800-millimeter gun.

321
00:22:46,760 --> 00:22:50,620
It took 3,000 men six weeks to emplace the gun.

322
00:22:50,621 --> 00:22:54,200
It had a 500-man crew to fire the gun.

323
00:22:54,380 --> 00:22:56,400
It had a major general for a gun captain.

324
00:22:56,660 --> 00:23:04,820
Here is a tremendous amount of assets that you have to pour into a weapon that is that big.

325
00:23:06,660 --> 00:23:13,780
Dora weighed in at roughly 1,350 tons, by far the heaviest cannon ever produced.

326
00:23:14,480 --> 00:23:19,480
It was so huge that, like Big Bertha, it had to be moved by rail in sections

327
00:23:19,481 --> 00:23:21,340
and erected on sight.

328
00:23:23,120 --> 00:23:25,640
Dora stood an imposing 38 feet high,

329
00:23:26,040 --> 00:23:29,680
was 23 feet wide, and 141 feet long.

330
00:23:30,580 --> 00:23:32,480
Its shell, filled with high explosives,

331
00:23:33,020 --> 00:23:34,380
could weigh up to 7 tons

332
00:23:34,381 --> 00:23:36,620
and would leave cities in rubble.

333
00:23:38,160 --> 00:23:41,120
Propelled by a charge weighing almost 2 tons by itself,

334
00:23:41,320 --> 00:23:44,820
this massive projectile traveled as far as 24 miles

335
00:23:44,821 --> 00:23:45,760
to reach its target.

336
00:23:45,761 --> 00:23:54,040
It and another large gun from Gustav were used at the siege of Sevastopol in Russia.

337
00:23:54,041 --> 00:24:00,260
And that was their job, was to break up large concrete formations and fortifications.

338
00:24:00,261 --> 00:24:07,140
They did the job well, but aircraft using large bombs could probably have done just

339
00:24:07,141 --> 00:24:14,320
as well and would have freed a lot more men and a lot more technology, a lot more manufacturing

340
00:24:14,321 --> 00:24:16,780
capability that went into building them.

341
00:24:16,781 --> 00:24:21,460
The German army always had this love of giantism.

342
00:24:21,461 --> 00:24:23,200
And so whenever they had an opportunity

343
00:24:23,201 --> 00:24:25,300
to reduce a fortress, they would haul up

344
00:24:25,301 --> 00:24:29,560
these monstrous works of art, like Dora and Gustav

345
00:24:29,561 --> 00:24:33,140
in the siege of Sevastopol.

346
00:24:33,141 --> 00:24:36,180
While Germany was preoccupied with big guns,

347
00:24:36,181 --> 00:24:37,660
the Allies took a different approach

348
00:24:37,661 --> 00:24:40,960
to gaining canon supremacy.

349
00:24:40,961 --> 00:24:44,320
In 1943, after a three-year trial and error period,

350
00:24:44,960 --> 00:24:47,320
the Allies perfected the proximity fuse,

351
00:24:47,820 --> 00:24:51,380
an anti-aircraft weapon that enhanced the lethality of cannon artillery.

352
00:24:52,760 --> 00:25:00,040
What the proximity fuse did was you put a radio transmitter into the nose of the round.

353
00:25:00,600 --> 00:25:04,000
The transmitter puts out a signal,

354
00:25:04,880 --> 00:25:10,800
and when the round gets a return signal off the target, it would detonate the round.

355
00:25:10,960 --> 00:25:16,260
The fuse first saw action with the U.S. Navy in the Pacific Theater.

356
00:25:17,140 --> 00:25:21,220
It worked so well that Army personnel wanted the fuse to support field troops,

357
00:25:21,500 --> 00:25:23,480
in addition to attacking German aircraft.

358
00:25:24,940 --> 00:25:27,420
During the Battle of the Bulge, the United States is in trouble.

359
00:25:28,480 --> 00:25:33,000
So what's done is they take that proximity fuse and they give it to the field artillery.

360
00:25:34,640 --> 00:25:38,280
And with field artillery, now you can fire that round,

361
00:25:38,281 --> 00:25:42,320
and it will burst 30 meters above the ground every time.

362
00:25:44,440 --> 00:25:47,700
Detonating over the ground increased the potency of the charge

363
00:25:47,701 --> 00:25:52,020
because the area hit was larger than one struck by a traditional shell.

364
00:25:54,800 --> 00:25:57,180
Cannon artillery equipped with a proximity fuse

365
00:25:57,181 --> 00:26:00,040
was also used by the Navy's big guns in the Pacific.

366
00:26:02,340 --> 00:26:05,020
The USS Iowa class of naval warships.

367
00:26:05,021 --> 00:26:09,480
four iowa class ships were built

368
00:26:09,481 --> 00:26:20,000
these battleships were almost 900 feet long and equipped with nine mammoth 16-inch diameter

369
00:26:20,001 --> 00:26:23,920
bore cannons that were some of the largest ever on a warship

370
00:26:23,921 --> 00:26:32,740
these iowas were known as swimming pool makers because of the 200-foot diameter craters produced

371
00:26:32,741 --> 00:26:37,220
by their shells, the largest weighing 2,700 pounds.

372
00:26:40,340 --> 00:26:43,780
The Iowas contained a fire direction system consisting of

373
00:26:43,781 --> 00:26:47,620
gyroscopes and an unheard of onboard computing system.

374
00:26:47,621 --> 00:26:52,820
The Aiken Relay Calculator allowed the Navy to quickly

375
00:26:52,821 --> 00:27:00,180
compute and compensate for errors and ranges to be able to

376
00:27:00,181 --> 00:27:04,660
determine what a projectile would do. It was a natural marriage of the

377
00:27:04,661 --> 00:27:08,480
complexities of naval gunfire to the computer age.

378
00:27:10,640 --> 00:27:15,880
Nicknamed the Mark I, Aiken's computer allowed the USS Iowa-class battleships

379
00:27:15,881 --> 00:27:22,360
to fire and hit targets 23 nautical miles, about 26 and a half statute miles,

380
00:27:22,540 --> 00:27:29,720
away, with almost pinpoint accuracy. With aircraft carriers and the Iowa-class

381
00:27:29,721 --> 00:27:34,520
leading the way, the United States replaced Great Britain as Queen of the Seas.

382
00:27:36,980 --> 00:27:42,740
Unfortunately for the U.S., unmatched firepower on the water didn't translate to coast artillery

383
00:27:42,741 --> 00:27:45,626
preeminence. New technology forced the U.S.

384
00:27:45,627 --> 00:27:48,580
to reconsider its commitment to cannon-based

385
00:27:48,581 --> 00:27:52,829
harbor defenses. It was evident that in the U.S.

386
00:27:52,830 --> 00:27:56,900
that as far as we were concerned, aircraft and

387
00:27:56,901 --> 00:28:03,660
surface-to-surface guided missiles were going to be the weapons of the future that would take the

388
00:28:03,661 --> 00:28:05,710
place of coast artillery.

389
00:28:05,711 --> 00:28:11,940
And so our heavy coast artillery didn't last a decade past the World War II.

390
00:28:13,440 --> 00:28:19,200
By 1949, all the coastal artillery defenses were abandoned or disbanded,

391
00:28:19,860 --> 00:28:24,072
leaving only an assortment of iron monuments, like this one, at the U.S.

392
00:28:24,073 --> 00:28:25,300
Army Ordnance Museum.

393
00:28:26,900 --> 00:28:30,900
Even as one branch of military ceased wielding cannons,

394
00:28:30,901 --> 00:28:33,900
new possibilities opened up for cannons,

395
00:28:33,901 --> 00:28:36,900
and the wrath they would unleash on the world.

396
00:28:38,900 --> 00:28:41,900
The devastation in Japan that ended World War II

397
00:28:41,901 --> 00:28:44,900
altered the landscape in cannon technology,

398
00:28:44,901 --> 00:28:47,900
as scientists and military officials pondered

399
00:28:47,901 --> 00:28:51,900
how best to utilize cannons in the second half of the 20th century.

400
00:28:51,901 --> 00:28:58,260
Harnessing nuclear power was a global concern in the years following World War II.

401
00:28:59,320 --> 00:29:03,800
In the early 50s, the U.S. military worked with the U.S. Atomic Energy Commission

402
00:29:03,801 --> 00:29:06,060
to develop an atomic cannon.

403
00:29:08,560 --> 00:29:10,700
They're trying to get an atomic weapon on the battlefield.

404
00:29:11,220 --> 00:29:12,520
Seems like a good idea at the time.

405
00:29:13,380 --> 00:29:17,680
The problem of getting an atomic weapon on the battlefield, a tactical nuclear weapon,

406
00:29:17,681 --> 00:29:23,280
was that you could only shrink the warhead down so small.

407
00:29:24,000 --> 00:29:28,260
The smallest they could get it was about 11 inches, 280 millimeter.

408
00:29:29,260 --> 00:29:32,880
And so Atomic Annie comes into effect.

409
00:29:33,440 --> 00:29:36,080
A huge gun, a huge gun.

410
00:29:36,081 --> 00:29:40,380
It fired a 15 kiloton projectile 25 miles.

411
00:29:42,260 --> 00:29:47,660
Atomic Annie's nuclear warhead packed an astounding 15,000 ton charge.

412
00:29:47,680 --> 00:29:54,680
Ten of these warheads would equal the force that decimated Hiroshima.

413
00:29:54,681 --> 00:29:59,680
Atomic Annie was 40 feet long and weighed 85 tons.

414
00:29:59,681 --> 00:30:06,680
But what set her apart from other cannons was the new wrinkle of having debilitating nuclear capability.

415
00:30:06,681 --> 00:30:11,680
On March 25, 1953 in Frenchman Flats, Nevada,

416
00:30:11,681 --> 00:30:15,720
An atomic cannon was test fired for the first time in history.

417
00:30:18,360 --> 00:30:24,320
Following its successful test firing, a handful of atomic Annies were built and sent around the world.

418
00:30:24,321 --> 00:30:29,320
It gave the Army something to use in the nuclear race.

419
00:30:29,460 --> 00:30:33,260
The Army could say, see, we too have nuclear capability.

420
00:30:33,740 --> 00:30:36,400
We have this very big gun that will fire a nuclear shell.

421
00:30:36,401 --> 00:30:39,920
because you've got to remember 1950s,

422
00:30:40,080 --> 00:30:43,380
there was talk of not even needing armies anymore at all.

423
00:30:44,100 --> 00:30:45,760
You would just need the Air Force to come over,

424
00:30:45,860 --> 00:30:47,540
drop the nuclear bomb on the enemy,

425
00:30:48,340 --> 00:30:54,240
and then you'd have a few soldiers to march in and mop up.

426
00:30:55,300 --> 00:30:58,300
So the Army was looking at a way of staying in the game,

427
00:30:58,680 --> 00:31:01,620
and by having this nuclear shell, it was able to do so.

428
00:31:01,621 --> 00:31:05,180
If you really go out into Fort Sill and look at Tom McGannin,

429
00:31:05,181 --> 00:31:11,940
It is essentially a railroad gun that sits down on a firing base rather than stays on the railroad tracks.

430
00:31:12,400 --> 00:31:16,720
And rather than having railroad trucks underneath of it and pulled by an engine,

431
00:31:17,000 --> 00:31:22,160
it had two large motor power units that snagged onto it, picked it up, and towed it around.

432
00:31:24,640 --> 00:31:28,900
Atomic Annie's nuclear feature was just one of its never-before-seen qualities.

433
00:31:28,901 --> 00:31:35,020
scientists needed to develop a system that could sustain the force of a cannon that fired nuclear

434
00:31:35,021 --> 00:31:43,120
warheads so they improved upon a turn-of-the-century idea atomic canning does have a double recoil

435
00:31:43,121 --> 00:31:49,360
system and what that means is there is recoil the normal recoil system you see in a lot of guns

436
00:31:49,361 --> 00:31:56,260
they come this way but it also has one underneath so that it slides this way and and the reason for

437
00:31:56,261 --> 00:32:04,820
that is it softens the recoil of that thing so that it can be rapidly fired again if needed.

438
00:32:04,821 --> 00:32:10,620
The big problem with Atomic Ante and the guns Leiker was the same problem the Germans had.

439
00:32:11,260 --> 00:32:12,943
A battery was two guns.

440
00:32:12,944 --> 00:32:17,920
Normally a battery is six, but this is a large battery with two guns

441
00:32:17,921 --> 00:32:22,280
that takes about a day to emplace. Your range is 20 miles,

442
00:32:22,281 --> 00:32:28,420
and in fluid warfare sometimes you know they're going to move faster than that

443
00:32:28,421 --> 00:32:36,200
the other problem was of course you know the soviets were a lot of things but they weren't

444
00:32:36,201 --> 00:32:44,040
stupid so they always had this thing targeted anywhere it went they knew where it was

445
00:32:44,041 --> 00:32:51,640
these problems proved too much to overcome and atomic ante went the way of big bertha

446
00:32:51,641 --> 00:32:57,880
the paris gun and the dora gun this atomic ante now rests at the u.s army ordinance museum in

447
00:32:57,881 --> 00:33:05,720
aberdeen maryland in fact during the early 50s it was thought cannons and field armies in general

448
00:33:05,721 --> 00:33:10,360
would be unnecessary since you could launch missiles and keep soldiers out of harm's way

449
00:33:12,920 --> 00:33:18,040
so the missiles were what phased out atomic ante because they were quicker to set up they could go

450
00:33:18,041 --> 00:33:25,160
farther they could hit harder they were about as accurate so it was just it was as far as you could

451
00:33:25,161 --> 00:33:31,960
go with artillery and atomic weapons at the same time in order to compete against rockets and

452
00:33:31,961 --> 00:33:37,480
missiles developers increased the mobility and accuracy of cannons so that they could remain a

453
00:33:37,481 --> 00:33:42,760
viable option on the battlefield the korean and vietnam wars were the first opportunities for

454
00:33:42,761 --> 00:33:46,520
cannons to prove they could still get the job done.

455
00:33:46,521 --> 00:33:50,720
The big advance made in Vietnam was a thing called the

456
00:33:50,721 --> 00:33:58,460
firebase, where the engineers could go out with a little

457
00:33:58,461 --> 00:34:01,620
bulldozer and very quickly clear away a clearing in the

458
00:34:01,621 --> 00:34:02,560
jungle.

459
00:34:02,561 --> 00:34:06,500
And then you could fly in guns and support an infantry

460
00:34:06,501 --> 00:34:11,560
operation well within the artillery fam so that you never

461
00:34:11,561 --> 00:34:16,821
had infantry outside the range of artillery.

462
00:34:16,822 --> 00:34:22,440
So the mobility of the helicopter was exploited

463
00:34:22,441 --> 00:34:27,400
to carry in guns, build these fire bases, fire away.

464
00:34:27,401 --> 00:34:34,040
But the real issue of Vietnam was that the mobility and the adaptiveness that was demanded

465
00:34:34,041 --> 00:34:41,540
to survive caused the American forces, to include the artillery, to get out of a mindset

466
00:34:41,541 --> 00:34:46,860
that at least occurred since World War I of a linear battlefield.

467
00:34:46,861 --> 00:34:51,700
With Vietnam's ever-changing front line and hilly terrain, smaller, lighter, more mobile

468
00:34:51,701 --> 00:34:55,060
cannons were necessary.

469
00:34:55,061 --> 00:35:00,940
To help combat Vietnam's unique landscape, the U.S. developed a new cannon.

470
00:35:00,941 --> 00:35:07,480
In 1966, the M102, a self-propelled 105-millimeter cannon, was first deployed.

471
00:35:09,280 --> 00:35:19,700
The M102 was another 105 howitzer, but it had a base plate that was staked into the ground and allowed the gun to traverse 360 degrees.

472
00:35:20,500 --> 00:35:25,680
Another obvious advantage, it was designed to be sling-loaded under helicopters, and it was lighter.

473
00:35:26,220 --> 00:35:29,100
Therefore, it gave you a more deployable system.

474
00:35:30,940 --> 00:35:34,460
Vietnam showed military leaders that wars were fluid events,

475
00:35:34,760 --> 00:35:37,680
and you needed artillery that would best match the war environment.

476
00:35:38,620 --> 00:35:42,720
It also showed them that cannons and their projectiles needed to be more adaptable.

477
00:35:42,721 --> 00:35:48,180
And of course, the secret of that was the invention of precision munitions.

478
00:35:49,020 --> 00:35:53,840
When artillery becomes as precise as smart bombs,

479
00:35:54,440 --> 00:36:00,420
then we'll find ourselves into this new age of precision firepower, precision cannons.

480
00:36:00,940 --> 00:36:06,540
Mobile, lightweight, and now equipped with precision projectiles,

481
00:36:07,740 --> 00:36:10,240
cannons reclaimed a commanding role in the battlefield.

482
00:36:11,300 --> 00:36:17,200
But at least one more world leader would fall victim to his unabiding obsession with large cannons.

483
00:36:20,080 --> 00:36:24,020
Before he gained international recognition as a Middle East strongman,

484
00:36:24,360 --> 00:36:29,680
former Iraqi leader Saddam Hussein was intrigued by the idea of having a supergun.

485
00:36:30,940 --> 00:36:34,300
a cannon so large that he could fire it into space.

486
00:36:35,340 --> 00:36:40,020
In 1989, Hussein met a Canadian-born astrophysicist

487
00:36:40,021 --> 00:36:41,440
named Gerald Bull,

488
00:36:41,960 --> 00:36:44,140
and their joint vision began to take shape.

489
00:36:44,900 --> 00:36:47,560
Ever the bully, Hussein wanted to aim his supergun

490
00:36:47,561 --> 00:36:48,780
at his Israeli neighbors.

491
00:36:49,840 --> 00:36:53,920
The problem with that was, well, several problems.

492
00:36:54,320 --> 00:36:57,760
One, it was fixed in the position.

493
00:36:58,640 --> 00:37:00,920
So how many rounds are you going to be able to fire

494
00:37:00,921 --> 00:37:05,660
before the Israeli Air Force, which is a very good air force,

495
00:37:06,500 --> 00:37:10,540
finds the gun and bombs it into about 8 million pieces.

496
00:37:12,360 --> 00:37:15,740
Because the supergun was so large and shrouded in secrecy,

497
00:37:16,220 --> 00:37:18,760
it was shipped in sections from England where it was built

498
00:37:18,761 --> 00:37:22,620
to Iraq under the pretense of being petrochemical pipes.

499
00:37:23,880 --> 00:37:28,340
When complete, the cannon would be a nearly 200-yard-long supergun,

500
00:37:28,341 --> 00:37:33,300
firing a 600-kilogram projectile, roughly 700 miles.

501
00:37:34,780 --> 00:37:40,300
The components for the cannon came from factories in England, Spain, Holland, and Switzerland.

502
00:37:41,280 --> 00:37:47,680
Unfortunately for Hussein, British customs agents seized the final eight sections of the supergun in November 1990,

503
00:37:48,140 --> 00:37:49,520
before it could be finished.

504
00:37:50,360 --> 00:37:54,580
While Saddam Hussein won't be harboring dreams of a supergun anytime soon,

505
00:37:54,581 --> 00:37:58,740
the United States continues to upgrade its cannon technology.

506
00:38:00,380 --> 00:38:03,820
New cannons for possible future wars will be produced

507
00:38:03,821 --> 00:38:07,080
in the same place they've been produced since 1813.

508
00:38:08,040 --> 00:38:10,420
Watervliet Arsenal near Albany, New York.

509
00:38:12,020 --> 00:38:15,960
Watervliet is the oldest continuously active arsenal in the United States.

510
00:38:16,580 --> 00:38:21,200
It is the only arsenal in the country making large caliber cannons for today's military.

511
00:38:21,201 --> 00:38:26,880
We're not here to build and produce a piece of metal that just goes and sits somewhere.

512
00:38:27,020 --> 00:38:37,200
We're here to build a cannon, an artillery piece that will work, that will protect us and our soldiers, and also not malfunction.

513
00:38:39,640 --> 00:38:46,120
The process of manufacturing today's cannons is a continuation of the art form started in the 13th century.

514
00:38:46,121 --> 00:38:56,180
The rotary forge is set up so that you take a preform of this gunmetal steel, which is maybe 8, 10 feet long.

515
00:38:56,740 --> 00:39:05,700
We take that and we put it into an induction furnace that heats that preform up to about 2,000, 2,100 degrees.

516
00:39:07,120 --> 00:39:12,400
After it's taken out of the furnace, the barrel travels on a conveyor belt toward the rotary forge.

517
00:39:12,401 --> 00:39:22,540
This huge cannon will begin to turn and move into the jaws of the rotary forge.

518
00:39:23,040 --> 00:39:30,080
The rotary forge has four large hammers, and those hammers are controlled by a computer control room.

519
00:39:31,820 --> 00:39:37,120
The size of the cannon barrel determines how much force the hammers will use to shape the barrel.

520
00:39:37,121 --> 00:39:41,960
Any time you heat up steel, you lose some of its properties.

521
00:39:42,400 --> 00:39:45,720
So what you have to do is heat it up again and instantly cool it.

522
00:39:46,280 --> 00:39:49,300
You'll put it through this annealing process,

523
00:39:50,020 --> 00:39:55,900
which if you cool it instantly, it returns all of its metal properties to it.

524
00:39:57,600 --> 00:40:00,760
This entire process takes about 15 minutes,

525
00:40:01,240 --> 00:40:04,220
down from the 15 hours it took only 20 years ago.

526
00:40:06,940 --> 00:40:11,300
The U.S. Navy is also taking advantage of improvements in cannon technology.

527
00:40:12,400 --> 00:40:16,000
The newly commissioned USS Winston Churchill is the first

528
00:40:16,001 --> 00:40:19,720
destroyer equipped with air gum, the extended range guided

529
00:40:19,721 --> 00:40:22,040
munition system.

530
00:40:22,041 --> 00:40:26,360
It's a rocket assisted 5 inch, 120 pound projectile with a

531
00:40:26,361 --> 00:40:29,220
range of 60 miles.

532
00:40:29,221 --> 00:40:32,360
This range is an increase by more than 50%

533
00:40:32,361 --> 00:40:34,920
over current destroyers.

534
00:40:34,921 --> 00:40:38,820
While that's impressive, field cannons are even more imposing

535
00:40:38,821 --> 00:40:41,980
as they become more lethal and autonomous.

536
00:40:41,981 --> 00:40:47,300
The high-tech capabilities of today's Paladin M109A6 have proven that.

537
00:40:47,940 --> 00:40:51,040
But despite the Paladin's innovative automatic firing system

538
00:40:51,041 --> 00:40:54,540
and its future compatibility with precision-guided weapons,

539
00:40:54,960 --> 00:40:57,120
such as the Excalibur XM982,

540
00:40:57,580 --> 00:40:59,900
there is still some talk in military circles

541
00:40:59,901 --> 00:41:02,880
about cannons becoming artillery dinosaurs.

542
00:41:05,580 --> 00:41:11,320
In this contest to determine which is the optimum weapon to use

543
00:41:11,321 --> 00:41:15,440
for fire support in the future, whether it's going to be a rocket-based or a cannon-based system,

544
00:41:15,960 --> 00:41:22,960
it really comes down to what system best supports the changing nature of war.

545
00:41:24,360 --> 00:41:30,260
The precision revolution and the information revolution all are going to change the way armies fight.

546
00:41:30,261 --> 00:41:37,420
The battlefield of, say, 2015 or 2020 is going to be an enormously more lethal battlefield

547
00:41:37,421 --> 00:41:40,280
where armies will be spread over greater and greater distances,

548
00:41:40,660 --> 00:41:42,940
and weapons, because of their precision,

549
00:41:43,400 --> 00:41:45,960
are going to be used more sparingly in the future.

550
00:41:47,240 --> 00:41:51,340
However clean the battlefield of the future may or may not become,

551
00:41:52,000 --> 00:41:55,840
at some level, war is likely to remain a hands-on business

552
00:41:55,841 --> 00:42:00,280
in which victory can only be won through the nitty-gritty of a direct showdown.

553
00:42:02,320 --> 00:42:05,680
Ultimately, combat culminates in a face-to-face contact

554
00:42:05,681 --> 00:42:08,580
between two maneuvering forces as they close on each other.

555
00:42:09,220 --> 00:42:12,580
And the longer you can fire against an enemy, the closer he gets,

556
00:42:12,780 --> 00:42:15,020
the more effective you're going to be in the close fight.

557
00:42:15,021 --> 00:42:19,540
And the best instrument for shooting close has always been

558
00:42:19,541 --> 00:42:22,200
and will continue to be cannon artillery.

559
00:42:24,020 --> 00:42:27,560
Cannons continue to play a crucial role in the art of war.

560
00:42:28,220 --> 00:42:30,640
Whether in visceral hand-to-hand combat

561
00:42:30,641 --> 00:42:33,800
or in the hands-off firing of precision weapons,

562
00:42:33,801 --> 00:42:40,800
They have maintained their position as the centerpiece of artillery for more than 600 years.

563
00:42:40,801 --> 00:42:51,800
Even in the face of newfound technology, it seems a safe bet that cannons will be here as long as wars are fought and battles need to be won.

564
00:42:58,800 --> 00:43:01,800
There are always going to be surprises.

565
00:43:01,801 --> 00:43:06,800
You don't have to speak the language to be able to interact with people.

566
00:43:06,801 --> 00:43:10,800
Knowing that can make you look at history in a new and exciting way.

